  "body": {
    "id": "prop:attainable-inclusion",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "The concrete attainable model satisfies \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{att}}\\subset\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}}\\).  In particular, for every \\(Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{att}}\\), its uniform design obeys\n\\[\n \\underline f\\le f_Q(x)\\le\\overline f\n\\]\nfor Lebesgue-almost every \\(x\\in\\mathcal X\\), including throughout the anchor neighborhood.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "AttClass",
      "FullClass",
      "flow",
      "f",
      "fup",
      "Qfull",
      "calX"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:uniform-design",
      "def:full-law-class",
      "def:attainable-law-class"
    ],
    "justification": "This inclusion makes the upper theorem a genuine result on a fixed positive-density submodel rather than an estimator-defined class.",
    "gap": "KennedyBalakrishnanRobinsWasserman2024 states high-level Gram and density-pilot conditions; the proposition verifies them directly for a named uniform-design submodel.",
    "consumer": "The inclusion supplies an auditable benchmark for implementations that randomize or reweight enrollment to an approximately uniform local design.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{att}}\\).  Every defining condition of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}}\\), except the two density inequalities, is already a defining condition of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{att}}\\).  Uniform design gives \\(f_Q=1\\) Lebesgue-almost everywhere.  Since \\(\\underline f\\le1\\le\\overline f\\),\n\\[\n f_Q(x)\\le\\overline f\\quad\\text{on }\\mathcal X,\n \\qquad\n f_Q(x)\\ge\\underline f\\quad\\text{on }x_0+[-r_0,r_0]^d\n\\]\nup to Lebesgue-null sets.  Hence \\(Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}}\\), proving \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{att}}\\subset\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{full}}\\).",
